title: Deleting an item
tags: Trash
kind: guide
summary: Delete deletes what is designated with no question: the file to the trash with its metarecords, or the metarecord when there is no file — never a file without its metarecord.


<<key "item:delete" Delete>> runs <<cmd "item:delete">> on what the focused panel
designates — a row of the [[metarecord-list panel]] or of the
[[file-manager panel]], the metarecord the [[metarecord-detail panel]] or the
[[viewer panel]] shows, a node of the [[path-ref panel]]:

* ''a file'', tracked or not, goes to the repository's trash, its metarecords with it ([[What trashing does]]);
* ''a metarecord without a file'' is deleted.

A file is never deleted without its metarecord: one that would stay would
describe a file that is gone. There is no question, since nothing here is final:
the trash gives the file and its metarecords back, and <<key "log:undo" Ctrl+z>>
brings a deleted metarecord back ([[The undo selection]]). For the same reason
it does nothing without an active repository — there would be no trash to
restore from.

!! Several at once

* `item:delete selection` — the checked metarecords;
* `item:delete query` — everything the metarecord list shows.

The metarecords without a file are deleted first, then the files of the others
go to the trash, their metarecords with them. In that order, a file the trash
refuses — another metarecord points at it — keeps its metarecord.

!! The narrower forms

|!To |!Command |
|delete the metarecord alone, its file kept |<<key "metarecord:delete" Shift+Delete>> — <<cmd "metarecord:delete">> |
|send the file to the trash, whatever the panel |<<cmd "file:trash">>, no key ([[File commands]]) |
|delete a trash entry for good |<<key "trash:prune entry" Delete>> in the [[trash panel]] — <<cmd "trash:prune">> |

The [[duplicates panel]] keeps its own Delete, `duplicates:trash`: it trashes
the copy under its cursor and re-counts the group at once, which a reload would
get wrong until the watcher has settled.
